# PROMPT 1 — Distance & Travel Time Sheet

> **Use when:** You have one starting point and a list of destinations (block HQs, town centres, admin offices, etc.) and want distances + travel times in Excel.

```
I need an Excel sheet (.xlsx) calculating road distances and travel times 
from one origin to a list of destinations.

## Origin
Coordinates: [LAT],[LON]
Label: [NAME OF ORIGIN LOCATION — e.g. "District Collector Office, Balrampur"]

## Destinations
[PASTE YOUR TABLE HERE — one row per destination, with at minimum:
 Name | Latitude | Longitude
 You can also include: District | Division | State | any other columns]

## Geography context
Country: [e.g. India]
Region: [e.g. Uttar Pradesh — Devipatan Division]
Road type: [e.g. "rural district roads" / "mix of NH and state highways" / "urban"]

## Calculation method
- Straight-line distance: Haversine formula (great-circle)
- Road distance: straight-line × [SINUOSITY FACTOR — use 1.3 for rural India, 
  1.2 for urban India, 1.15 for Europe, 1.25 for rural USA; or state your own]
- Average speed: [SPEED in km/h — use 40 for rural India, 60 for state highways, 
  30 for urban, 80 for motorways]
- Formula: travel time = road distance ÷ speed

## Output specification
Single Excel tab named "Distance & Travel Time". Columns in this exact order:
  A  — Row number
  B  — Destination name
  C  — [Any grouping column — e.g. District / County / Province]
  D  — [Any second grouping column — e.g. Division / State]
  E  — Latitude (4 decimal places)
  F  — Longitude (4 decimal places)
  G  — Straight-line distance (km, 1 decimal)
  H  — Estimated road distance (km, 1 decimal)
  I  — Estimated travel time (format: "45m" or "1h 20m")
  J  — "Get Directions" hyperlink opening Google Maps from origin to destination

## Styling rules (follow exactly)
- Header row: dark background (#2D3748), white bold Arial 10, height 30
- Data rows: alternate white (#FFFFFF) and light grey (#F7F6F2)
- Travel time column (I): colour-code cells —
    green  (#D1FAE5, font #065F46) = under 30 minutes
    amber  (#FEF3C7, font #92400E) = 30 to 60 minutes
    red    (#FEE2E2, font #991B1B) = over 60 minutes
- If destinations span multiple districts/counties: colour-band the grouping 
  column (column C) with a distinct light tint per group
- Sort rows: nearest to farthest (ascending by column G)
- Freeze row 1 (header); apply auto-filter
- Add an AVERAGE row at the bottom for columns G and H
- Add a legend below the data: travel time colour key + grouping colour key
- Rows 1–3: title, origin label + coordinates, methodology note (italic grey)
- Number formats: G and H → "0.0"; E and F → "0.0000"

## Hyperlink formula pattern
=HYPERLINK("https://www.google.com/maps/dir/[ORIGIN_LAT],[ORIGIN_LON]/[DEST_LAT],[DEST_LON]","Get Directions")

## After building
1. Save to disk
2. Run formula recalculation to verify 0 errors
3. Deliver the file for download

Do not add extra sheets. Do not add a map. Just the one clean sheet.
```

