# PROMPT 2 — Administrative Unit Workbook

> **Use when:** You want a comprehensive Excel workbook documenting an administrative hierarchy — e.g. a country's states, a state's districts, a district's blocks/tehsils.

```
Create a comprehensive Excel workbook (.xlsx) for the administrative 
geography of [REGION NAME — e.g. "Uttar Pradesh, India" / "Bavaria, Germany" 
/ "Oyo State, Nigeria"].

## Administrative levels to include
Level 1 (top): [e.g. Division / Prefecture / Province] — [COUNT if known]
Level 2 (mid): [e.g. District / County / LGA] — [COUNT if known]
Level 3 (bottom): [e.g. Block / Tehsil / Ward / Commune] — [COUNT if known]

## Data to include per Level 2 unit (one row each)
- Name
- Parent unit (Level 1)
- Area in km² (from official census or best available source)
- Total population (state the census year)
- Population density (persons per km²) — calculate: population ÷ area
- Count of Level 3 sub-units
- Total urban/municipal area (km²) — sum of all city/town jurisdictions
- Urban coverage % — calculate: urban area ÷ total area
- Largest city / municipal body name
- City jurisdiction type (e.g. Municipal Corporation / Nagar Palika / Town Council)

## Data to include per Level 3 unit (one row each, separate sheet)
- Name
- Parent Level 2 unit
- Parent Level 1 unit
- Approximate latitude of HQ (4 decimal places)
- Approximate longitude of HQ (4 decimal places)
- Google Maps hyperlink: =HYPERLINK("https://maps.google.com/?q=LAT,LON","View on Map")

## Sheets required (in this order)
Sheet 1 — "[Level 2] Summary"
  Columns: Name | Level1 | Area km² | Population | Density /km² | 
           [Level3] Count | Urban Area km² | Urban % | Largest City | City Type
  One row per Level 2 unit, sorted alphabetically
  Totals / averages row at the bottom
  Auto-filter on header; freeze header row

Sheet 2 — "Municipal Bodies"
  Columns: [Level2 Name] | [Level1 Name] | Municipal Body | Body Type | 
           Area km² | [Level2] Total Area km² | Coverage %
  One row per city/town (districts with multiple cities = multiple rows)
  Coverage % = HYPERLINK formula column: area ÷ district area
  Auto-filter; freeze header

Sheet 3 — "[Level1] Summary"  
  Rollup by Level 1 unit:
  Columns: Level1 Name | [Level2] Count | Total Area km² | Total Population | 
           Avg Density /km² | Total [Level3] Count | Total Urban km²
  Totals row at bottom

Sheet 4 — "[Level3] — Lat & Lon"
  One row per Level 3 unit with lat/lon and Maps hyperlink
  Columns: # | Name | [Level2] | [Level1] | Latitude | Longitude | View on Map
  Auto-filter; freeze header
  After building this sheet, verify unit counts per Level 2 match 
  official census totals. State any discrepancies clearly.

## Data quality rules
- State the census year for all population and area data
- Mark coordinates as "approximate centroids — GIS orientation only"
- If a Level 2 boundary changed after the census (new unit carved out), 
  use POST-reorganisation boundaries and note the change year
- Do not list Level 3 units under the old parent if they were 
  transferred to a new unit after reorganisation
- Flag any count you are uncertain about with "(unverified)" 

## Styling rules
- Each sheet: no gridlines, Arial 10 font throughout
- Headers: dark background, white bold text, height 30
- Alternating row fills: white / very light grey
- Density column: colour-band cells by tier:
    < 100 /km²  → lightest blue
    100–500      → light blue  
    500–1000     → medium blue
    1000–2000    → dark blue
    > 2000       → darkest blue (white font)
- Totals/averages row: light warm grey (#E8E4DC), bold

## After building
1. Save to disk
2. Run formula recalculation — must report 0 errors
3. Deliver the file for download
```

