# Reference — Sinuosity Factors and Speed Assumptions

Use these to fill in Prompt 1. Adjust based on what you know about your geography.

## Road sinuosity factor (straight-line → road distance multiplier)

| Road type | Factor | Notes |
|-----------|--------|-------|
| Rural India (district roads) | **1.30** | Winding roads, few bypasses |
| Rural India (state highways) | **1.20** | Straighter but still indirect |
| Urban India | **1.25** | Grid + one-ways |
| Rural sub-Saharan Africa | **1.40** | More indirect routing |
| Rural Europe | **1.20** | Better road network |
| Urban Europe | **1.15** | Denser grid |
| Rural USA/Canada | **1.15** | Highway-centric planning |
| Urban USA | **1.20** | Grid cities |
| Mountainous terrain (any) | **1.50–1.70** | Add 0.2–0.4 for hills |

## Average speed assumptions

| Road type | Speed |
|-----------|-------|
| Rural India — district roads | **40 km/h** |
| Rural India — state highway | **60 km/h** |
| Urban India | **25 km/h** |
| Rural Africa — unpaved | **30 km/h** |
| Rural Europe — country road | **70 km/h** |
| European motorway | **110 km/h** |
| US rural highway | **90 km/h** |
| US urban | **40 km/h** |

> **Note:** These are journey-average speeds, not road speed limits. They account for stops, junctions, and slow sections.

