# What will cause Claude to hallucinate — and how to prevent it

## Hallucination risk #1 — Administrative reorganisation
**Risk:** Claude lists sub-units under the old parent district/county when they were transferred to a newly created unit.  
**Fix:** Tell Claude: *"[Region X] was carved out of [Region Y] in [Year]. Do not list [Region X]'s units under [Region Y]."*

## Hallucination risk #2 — Approximate coordinates presented as precise
**Risk:** Claude generates coordinates that look like GPS readings but are estimated centroids.  
**Fix:** Always label coordinates in the sheet as "approximate centroids — not survey-grade." Use the Maps hyperlink to verify each one.

## Hallucination risk #3 — Plausible but wrong unit counts
**Risk:** Claude generates a list of sub-units that looks complete but is missing entries or has duplicates under variant spellings.  
**Fix:** Tell Claude the expected count. Say: *"[Region] should have exactly [N] blocks. Verify your list matches this. Flag any discrepancy."*

## Hallucination risk #4 — Unit names with spelling variants
**Risk:** Claude generates the same place under two different spellings as separate rows.  
**Fix:** After the first draft, ask: *"Scan column B for duplicate place names under different spellings. List any you find."*

## Hallucination risk #5 — Wrong data year mixing
**Risk:** Claude silently mixes census data from different years (e.g. 2001 area, 2011 population).  
**Fix:** Specify: *"All data must be from [YEAR] census. If a figure is from a different year, say so explicitly in parentheses."*

## Hallucination risk #6 — Made-up boundary URLs
**Risk:** Claude invents a plausible-sounding CDN URL that doesn't exist.  
**Fix:** Either provide the URL yourself (from the reference table above), or say: *"Only use boundary URLs you can verify are in your training data. If unsure, state the source name and let me find the URL."*

